# Background & Objectives

## Scientific Rationale

The neonatal Fc receptor (FcRn) salvages IgG antibodies from lysosomal degradation, recycling them into circulation. In gMG, pathogenic anti-AChR and anti-MuSK IgG antibodies impair neuromuscular junction transmission. FcRn inhibition with efgartigimod accelerates IgG catabolism, reducing all IgG subclasses including pathogenic autoantibodies — a mechanism validated across gMG, ITP, CIDP, and pemphigus vulgaris.

**Key pharmacology:**

- FcRn blockade → reduced IgG half-life → accelerated IgG catabolism
- ~50–75% total IgG reduction at steady state (dose-dependent)
- Onset within 1–2 weeks; reversible upon discontinuation
- Disease-modifying: clinical improvement correlates with IgG reduction magnitude

## Study Objectives

- Characterise baseline transcriptomic and proteomic profiles that distinguish responders from non-responders at week 12
- Quantify IgG_total NPX dynamics using an Emax pharmacodynamic framework
- Build and evaluate ML classifiers for week-12 response prediction from baseline multi-omics data
- Identify cross-modal transcriptomic–proteomic signatures for companion diagnostic development

